From af0e0808ae1500913e0e8da4ebbbb04e55c976a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Dodsley <simon@purestorage.com>
Date: Fri, 10 Jul 2026 10:43:04 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Pure Storage: check SDK response on volume/snapshot
 operations

The py-pure-client SDK does not raise on API errors; it returns a
response object with a status code and errors. Several of the Pure
driver's write operations called the SDK but discarded the response, so
a failure was never detected.

The most visible case is create_volume: when a FlashArray has reached
its maximum volume count, post_volumes() returns HTTP 400 with "limit
reached" rather than creating the volume. Because the response was
ignored, the volume was reported to Cinder as 'available' even though it
did not exist on the array, and the failure only surfaced later on
attach/snapshot/delete. The same silent-success pattern affected other
operations that reach an array limit or otherwise fail.

Add a _raise_if_failed() helper that inspects an SDK response and raises
PureDriverException on a 400, with a dedicated message when an array
limit has been reached. Wire it into the affected create/modify paths:
create_volume, create_volume_from_snapshot, create_cloned_volume,
create_with_qos, create_in_vgroup, create_from_snap_in_vgroup,
create_snapshot, extend_volume, revert_to_snapshot,
create_consistencygroup, create_cgsnapshot, the consistency group clone
and the replication failover volume create.

Checking the response also surfaced a pre-existing bug in
revert_to_snapshot: it passed the snapshot name as the target volume and
the volume name as the source, so the array rejected the request with an
invalid-name error that was previously ignored, making revert a silent
no-op. The arguments are now in the correct order so revert overwrites
the volume with the snapshot contents.

Because these operations now raise where they previously continued
silently, two create paths could leave orphaned backend resources behind
on a mid-operation failure. create_consistencygroup now tears down the
protection group it created if the subsequent replication configuration
fails, and _create_cg_from_cg rolls back any volumes it has already
cloned before re-raising (the temporary group snapshot was already
removed in a finally block). Other best-effort cleanup paths
(delete/eradicate) are intentionally left unchanged.

Closes-Bug: #2160366

